{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01965AWTvtwDc3UKtgMovt5G","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I found an excellent example. Here are the full contents of both files from the `cmd/cat` utility:\n\n## cmd/cat/main.go\n\nThe file demonstrates several key patterns:\n\n1. **Copyright header** (lines 1-2):\n   ```\n   // Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n   // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n   ```\n\n2. **Package structure**: Standard `package main` with descriptive comment explaining what the command does\n\n3. **sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() usage** (line 29):\n   - Called at the very start of main()\n   - Handles broken pipe errors gracefully\n\n4. **Write error handling** (lines 43-44):\n   - Explicitly checks for EPIPE errors with `errors.Is(err, syscall.EPIPE)`\n   - Exits with code 0 when EPIPE occurs (expected behavior for pipes)\n   - Other errors trigger exit code 1\n\n5. **Error handling patterns**:\n   - Uses `errors.Is()` and `errors.As()` for type-safe error checking\n   - Formats path errors using `formatErr()` helper\n   - Reports errors to stderr with context\n\n6. **Buffered I/O**: Uses `bufio.Reader` and `bufio.Writer` for efficient streaming with explicit `Flush()` calls to detect write errors\n\n## cmd/cat/cat_test.go\n\nThe test file demonstrates:\n\n1. **Test structure**:\n   - Uses `testutils.BuildBinary(t, \".\")` to compile the binary for testing\n   - Looks up reference binary with `exec.LookPath()`\n\n2. **testutils.RunDiffTests pattern** (lines 42 and 57):\n   - Creates `[]testutils.DiffTest` slices with test cases\n   - Each test case specifies: `Name`, `Args`, `Stdin`, and optional `WorkDir`\n   - Compares output of the Go implementation against a reference implementation (`gcat`)\n\n3. **Test coverage includes**:\n   - Default stdin reading\n   - Binary passthrough\n   - Flag combinations (-n, -b, -s, -v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t, -u)\n   - Multi-file handling\n   - Error cases (missing files with partial success)\n   - SIGPIPE handling with a specific test that pipes to a closed stdout\n\n4. **Helper functions**:\n   - `allBytes()`: Generates test data covering all byte values\n   - `writeFixture()`: Creates temporary test files with content\n\nThe test file is comprehensive and validates both normal and edge cases, particularly around signal handling and write error scenarios."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4803ee21-8ebf-4ff1-ae3b-2b34c42a3c29","uuid":"ffb26801-1cdc-4f67-b344-93f55cf3f634","timestamp":"2026-05-15T02:34:57.325Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5246, find a simple cmd/ utility that has both main.go and a _test.go file already generated. I need to see the coding patterns used: copyright header, package structure, how they use sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler, how they handle write errors, and the test file structure with testutils.RunDiffTests. Look at a few cmd/ directories to find one that exists and has code. Try cmd/yes, cmd/true, cmd/false, cmd/cat, cmd/basename, cmd/dirname, cmd/realpath. Read both the main.go and test file of whichever one you find. Report the full contents of both files.","agentId":"af55a85c644460c6a","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I found an excellent example. Here are the full contents of both files from the `cmd/cat` utility:\n\n## cmd/cat/main.go\n\nThe file demonstrates several key patterns:\n\n1. **Copyright header** (lines 1-2):\n   ```\n   // Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n   // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n   ```\n\n2. **Package structure**: Standard `package main` with descriptive comment explaining what the command does\n\n3. **sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() usage** (line 29):\n   - Called at the very start of main()\n   - Handles broken pipe errors gracefully\n\n4. **Write error handling** (lines 43-44):\n 